::CW_U06_PRE_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Pretest item. Which action best begins integrated portfolio preparation?{
~A. Decorate the portfolio before checking contents.#Incorrect. First check completeness and purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Submit the first file found.#Incorrect. Selection should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. List required parts and mark each present, missing, or needing revision.#Correct. This gives a clear readiness map. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Remove the author statement.#Incorrect. Reflection is part of portfolio synthesis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which piece belongs in a final portfolio?{
=A. A revised piece that shows a specific craft skill and reader effect.#Correct. This supports portfolio evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. A piece with no title and no revision.#Incorrect. This is not reader-ready. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A favorite piece the writer cannot explain.#Incorrect. Preference alone is weak evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A draft with comments still visible.#Incorrect. Draft comments weaken final readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q03::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A portfolio statement discusses voice. Which evidence best matches it?{
~A. A random image pasted beside the title.#Incorrect. This does not show voice development. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A revised story excerpt where dialogue and sentence rhythm reveal character voice.#Correct. This directly supports the stated craft growth.
~C. A file name with no writing attached.#Incorrect. A file name is not craft evidence.
~D. A paragraph saying the writer worked hard.#Incorrect. Effort alone does not show voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which portfolio order has the clearest purpose?{
~A. Alphabetical by file name with no explanation.#Incorrect. This may not guide craft understanding. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Random order based on download time.#Incorrect. Random order does not support readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Longest to shortest only because length matters most.#Incorrect. Length is not the main purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Story first for revised dialogue, poem second for voice through rhythm, statement last to explain growth.#Correct. This order guides the reader through evidence.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which note is most useful during a readiness pass?{
=A. Preview script and make character labels consistent.#Correct. This names a specific publication-readiness action.
~B. Fix it later.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Maybe change something.#Incorrect. This does not identify the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It looks fine from far away.#Incorrect. Readiness requires specific checks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q06::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] How should an author statement connect to selected pieces?{
~A. It should discuss unrelated goals only.#Incorrect. The statement should connect to the work.
~B. It should hide all revision evidence.#Incorrect. Revision evidence supports growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. It should name skills the reader can see in the portfolio.#Correct. The statement and evidence should support each other.
~D. It should replace the creative pieces.#Incorrect. It explains the pieces; it does not replace them.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q07::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which presentation choice best supports the portfolio?{
~A. Read the longest excerpt regardless of purpose.#Incorrect. Excerpt choice should be focused. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Avoid explaining the excerpt.#Incorrect. The presentation needs craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Summarize every assignment from the course.#Incorrect. That is too broad. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Choose one excerpt that shows a craft choice connected to portfolio growth.#Correct. This connects presentation and portfolio evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q08::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which problem should be fixed before final publication?{
~A. The writer can explain the piece clearly.#Incorrect. That is a strength, not a problem.
=B. The final file includes bracketed notes that say add ending later.#Correct. Draft notes show the piece is not final.
~C. The portfolio has a clear title.#Incorrect. A clear title supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The author statement names revision growth.#Incorrect. That supports synthesis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q09::[MLA.CW.REV.01] What makes a portfolio integrated?{
~A. The files are unrelated but colorful.#Incorrect. Visual decoration does not create integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The portfolio has no explanation.#Incorrect. Author reasoning is needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The selected pieces, statement, revision evidence, and presentation plan connect to shared growth.#Correct. Integration means the parts work together. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The presentation ignores the pieces.#Incorrect. The presentation should connect to the portfolio.
}

::CW_U06_PRE_Q10::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which answer best explains reader effect?{
=A. I revised the line breaks so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names a choice and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I put this first because I found it first.#Incorrect. This does not explain reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I added random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not support readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I like the color.#Incorrect. Preference is not reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
